## Production Checklist

| Concern | How it's addressed |
|---|---|
| **State durability** | `PostgresSaver` — checkpoints survive restarts, work across replicas |
| **Multi-tenancy** | `threadId = userId:conversationId`; auth identity from JWT, never from client input |
| **Memory across turns** | `releaseThread(false)` + checkpointer = automatic conversation history |
| **Runaway loops** | The ReAct loop is bounded — also set request timeouts on your HTTP client and a max-token limit in chat options |
| **Tool failures** | Tools return error text so the LLM can recover; business exceptions never leak to users |
| **Observability** | Actuator + Prometheus; add `langgraph4j-opentelemetry` for per-node tracing; log each `step.node()` |
| **Auditability** | `graph.getStateHistory(config)` replays every step of any conversation |
| **Secrets** | All keys/passwords via environment variables |
| **Testing** | Graph is deterministic infrastructure; mock the model and business services |

## Two upgrades to consider next

1. **Human-in-the-loop for refunds** — switch to `AgentExecutorEx` and add `interruptBefore("requestRefund")` so refund tool calls pause the graph until a supervisor approves via a separate endpoint (`graph.invoke(null, configWithCheckpointId)` resumes it). The Postgres checkpoint makes the pause survivable for days.
2. **Token streaming** — pass a streaming-enabled model (`.chatModel(model, true)`) and expose an SSE endpoint (`text/event-stream`) that forwards `StreamingOutput` chunks for a ChatGPT-style typing effect.

This structure — *graph compiled once as a bean, durable saver, thread-per-conversation, tools as Spring components* — is the pattern the LangGraph4j maintainers themselves use in the `spring-ai-agent` reference module, and it scales horizontally out of the box since all state lives in Postgres.
